# Architecture selection and hardware capabilities.
#
# ARCH_HAS_* symbols are readonly: they describe what the hardware can do, not what
# to build. An architecture asserts them with `select`, and everything else depends
# on them. This is what lets the config system know that SMP is impossible on a
# Cortex-M without anyone writing that rule down twice.
#
# Each one corresponds to a capability trait in the `hal` crate; see
# docs/portability.md.

config ARCH_HAS_MMU
    bool
    readonly
    help
        The target has a hardware MMU with page tables.
        Corresponds to the HasMmu trait.

config ARCH_HAS_FLAT
    bool
    readonly
    help
        The port can run with address translation off, so MM_FLAT is a
        memory model it offers. Every target without an MMU has this and
        nothing else; an MMU target has it only if its port carries a path
        that never builds page tables.

        x86_64 and AArch64 do not. Long mode cannot run with paging off at
        all, and the AArch64 port maps its UART and every device window
        through DEVICE_WINDOW_BASE from code that is not conditional on the
        memory model. i686 does: it builds flat today, which is why this is
        a property of the port rather than of having an MMU.

config ARCH_HAS_MPU
    bool
    readonly
    help
        The target has a memory protection unit but no address translation.

config ARCH_HAS_SMP
    bool
    readonly
    help
        The target can execute more than one hardware thread.

config ARCH_HAS_CAS
    bool
    readonly
    help
        The target has atomic compare-and-swap at machine word width.
        Without it, lock-free data structures are not offered at all.

        This records the port's claim. Source code does not gate atomics
        on it: an atomic method that the target lacks is a compile error
        even inside a function that is never called, so atomics are gated
        with `#[cfg(target_has_atomic = "N")]`, which is the compiler's own
        knowledge of the target. `kbuild portability` checks that gating.

config ARCH_HAS_COHERENT_DMA
    bool
    readonly
    help
        DMA-capable devices see coherent memory; no manual cache maintenance.

config ARCH_HAS_FPU
    bool
    readonly
    help
        Floating-point state exists and must be saved across context switches.

config ARCH_HAS_PMP
    bool
    readonly
    help
        The target has Physical Memory Protection that binds the mode the
        kernel runs in, so a stack guard can fault without an MMU. The
        riscv32 port locks a no-access region over each stack guard, which is
        what lets the stack-guard test modes run on a core with no page
        tables. ARMv7-M's MPU guards its stacks too, but proves it with boot
        probes rather than these test modes, so it does not select this.

choice ARCH
    prompt "Target architecture"
    default ARCH_X86_64

    config ARCH_X86_64
        bool "x86-64"
        select ARCH_HAS_MMU
        select ARCH_HAS_SMP
        select ARCH_HAS_CAS
        select ARCH_HAS_COHERENT_DMA
        select ARCH_HAS_FPU

    config ARCH_AARCH64
        bool "AArch64"
        select ARCH_HAS_MMU
        select ARCH_HAS_SMP
        select ARCH_HAS_CAS
        select ARCH_HAS_FPU

    config ARCH_I686
        bool "32-bit x86"
        select ARCH_HAS_MMU
        select ARCH_HAS_FLAT
        select ARCH_HAS_SMP
        select ARCH_HAS_CAS
        select ARCH_HAS_COHERENT_DMA
        select ARCH_HAS_FPU

    config ARCH_ARMV7M
        bool "ARMv7-M (Cortex-M, no MMU)"
        select ARCH_HAS_FLAT
        select ARCH_HAS_MPU
        # LDREX/STREX. ARMv6-M (Cortex-M0) has no exclusive access and
        # would be a separate choice without this select.
        select ARCH_HAS_CAS

    config ARCH_RISCV32
        bool "RISC-V 32-bit (no MMU)"
        select ARCH_HAS_FLAT
        # The A extension's LR/SC, unless the base-ISA variant below says
        # this core has none.
        select ARCH_HAS_CAS if !RISCV32_NO_ATOMICS
        # Machine-mode PMP, which `virt`'s harts have with and without the A
        # extension; the port locks a no-access region over each stack guard.
        select ARCH_HAS_PMP
endchoice

config RISCV32_NO_ATOMICS
    bool "rv32i: the base ISA, without the A extension"
    depends on ARCH_RISCV32
    default n
    help
        Build for a core with no atomic instructions at all: rv32i rather
        than rv32imac. The kernel then has no compare-and-swap, so it takes
        the uniprocessor lock family throughout, and every atomic item the
        portable crates gate on `target_has_atomic` is absent rather than
        stubbed.

        This is the configuration docs/portability.md has claimed since
        Phase 1 and the roadmap's Phase 4 exit requires: an image that boots
        on a machine whose hardware would refuse an atomic instruction.
        The preset runs on QEMU's `rv32` hart with the A extension switched
        off, and M and C with it, so an atomic, multiply, divide or compressed
        instruction is illegal. QEMU's `rv32i` model cannot be used: it has
        no Zicsr, and a machine-mode kernel cannot run without CSRs.

source "board.kcfg"
